  "grammar_points": [
    {
      "name": "〜なき (literary negation, 'without')",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N2",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: なき (naki) is the classical/literary form of ない (nai, 'without/not'), used to modify nouns. It gives a poetic, timeless quality.\n\nExample from this song: 終わりなき (owari naki) = 'endless / without end' — from 終わり (owari, 'end') + なき (naki, literary 'without')\n\nMore examples:\n• 果てなき旅 (hate naki tabi, 'an endless journey') — from 果て (hate, 'end')\n• 名もなき花 (na mo naki hana, 'a nameless flower') — from 名 (na, 'name')\n• 限りなき愛 (kagiri naki ai, 'limitless love') — from 限り (kagiri, 'limit')\n\nNote: なき is the literary equivalent of ない used as an adjective. Modern Japanese would say 終わりのない (owari no nai). なき appears in song titles, poetry, and formal writing for its elegance."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "終わり (owari, 'end') + なき (naki, literary 'without') → 終わりなき (owari naki, 'endless / without end')"
    },
    {
      "name": "〜かけて (on the verge of)",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N3",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: かける (kakeru) attached to a verb's masu-stem means 'about to,' 'on the verge of,' or 'half-done.' It expresses an incomplete or starting action.\n\nExample from this song: 掴みかけて (tsukami kakete) = 'on the verge of grasping' — from 掴む (tsukamu, 'to grasp') → 掴み (tsukami, stem) + かけて (kakete)\n\nMore examples:\n• 死にかける (shini kakeru, 'nearly die') — from 死ぬ (shinu, 'to die')\n• 言いかけて止めた (ii kakete yameta, 'started to say but stopped') — from 言う (iu, 'to say')\n• 食べかけのパン (tabe kake no pan, 'half-eaten bread') — from 食べる (taberu, 'to eat')\n\nNote: 〜かけ as a noun modifier means 'half-done': 読みかけの本 (yomikake no hon, 'half-read book'). The て-form かけて connects to the next action: 'was on the verge of X, and then...'"
      },
      "conjugation_path": "掴む (tsukamu, 'to grasp') → 掴み (tsukami, stem) + かけて (kakete, 'on the verge of') → 掴みかけて (tsukami kakete, 'on the verge of grasping')"
    },
    {
      "name": "けして〜ない (never)",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N3",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: けして (keshite) or 決して (keshite) combined with a negative verb means 'never' or 'by no means.' It creates an emphatic absolute negation.\n\nExample from this song: The song uses けして...ない to express something that will absolutely never happen — an unbreakable resolve.\n\nMore examples:\n• 決して忘れない (keshite wasurenai, 'I will never forget') — from 忘れる (wasureru, 'to forget')\n• 決して諦めない (keshite akiramenai, 'I will never give up') — from 諦める (akirameru, 'to give up')\n• 決して許さない (keshite yurusanai, 'I will never forgive') — from 許す (yurusu, 'to forgive')\n\nNote: 決して must always pair with a negative form. Never use it with positive verbs. It's stronger than ぜんぜん〜ない (zenzen ~nai, 'not at all'). 決して expresses firm determination."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "決して (keshite, 'never') + verb negative form → 決して〜ない (keshite ~nai, 'absolutely never')"
    },
    {
      "name": "〜ずに (without doing)",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N3",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: ずに (zu ni) is the classical negative te-form meaning 'without doing.' Replace ない with ず and add に to connect to the next action.\n\nExample from this song: 逸らさずに (sorasazu ni) = 'without averting (one's gaze)' — from 逸らす (sorasu, 'to avert') → 逸らさない (sorasanai) → 逸らさず (sorasazu) + に (ni)\n\nMore examples:\n• 食べずに寝た (tabezu ni neta, 'slept without eating') — from 食べる (taberu, 'to eat')\n• 迷わずに進む (mayowazu ni susumu, 'advance without hesitating') — from 迷う (mayou, 'to hesitate')\n• 諦めずに頑張る (akiramezu ni ganbaru, 'keep trying without giving up')\n\nNote: ずに = literary ないで. Exception: する → せずに (sezu ni), not しずに. ず can also stand alone without に in literary contexts: 言わず語らず (iwazu katarazu, 'without saying or telling')."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "逸らす (sorasu, 'to avert') → 逸らさない (sorasanai) → 逸らさず (sorasazu) + に (ni) → 逸らさずに (sorasazu ni, 'without averting')"
    },
    {
      "name": "〜ために (for the sake of)",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N4",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: ために (tame ni) expresses purpose ('in order to') or benefit ('for the sake of'). After a verb dictionary form, it means 'in order to do.' After a noun + の, it means 'for the sake of.'\n\nExample from this song: The song uses ために to express what the speaker is striving for — the purpose behind their actions.\n\nMore examples:\n• 合格するために勉強する (goukaku suru tame ni benkyou suru, 'study in order to pass')\n• 家族のために働く (kazoku no tame ni hataraku, 'work for the family')\n• 健康のために走る (kenkou no tame ni hashiru, 'run for health')\n\nNote: Verb + ために = 'in order to (do).' Noun + のために = 'for the sake of (noun).' Compare with ように, which is for non-volitional results."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "verb dictionary form + ために (tame ni, 'in order to') / noun + の + ために (no tame ni, 'for the sake of')"
    },
    {
      "name": "〜ていく (continuing into the future)",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N4",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: ていく (te iku) expresses an action or change continuing from now into the future. It shows forward progression through time.\n\nExample from this song: 生きていく (ikite iku) = 'will go on living' — from 生きる (ikiru, 'to live') → 生きて (ikite) + いく (iku, 'to go')\n\nMore examples:\n• 強くなっていく (tsuyoku natte iku, 'getting stronger and stronger')\n• 変わっていく (kawatte iku, 'keeps changing over time') — from 変わる (kawaru, 'to change')\n• 学んでいく (manande iku, 'will continue learning') — from 学ぶ (manabu, 'to learn')\n\nNote: ていく shows future-directed change. てくる shows past-to-present change. 生きていく is a powerful expression meaning 'I will go on living' — often used to express determination in the face of difficulty."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "生きる (ikiru, 'to live') → 生きて (ikite) + いく (iku, 'to go') → 生きていく (ikite iku, 'will go on living')"
    }
  ]
